 of your hand.\n\nSo your actual hand should only contain **T9** and possibly other singles.\n\nWait \u2014 you list hand as [T9:1; B2:1; B3:1; B4:1; B7:1], but also have melds using those exact tiles. That implies either:\n1. The game state representation allows melded tiles to appear in hand (incorrect).\n2. Or more likely: There's confusion between concealed and exposed sets.\n\nGiven standard rules: once a meld is formed via Chi/Peng/Gang, those tiles are moved to the front and are no longer in hand.\n\nFrom your history:\n- You performed \"Chi B3\" \u2192 meaning you used B3 to form a Chow, probably [B2,B3,B4], using discarded B3.\n- Later you did \"Chi B8\" \u2192 forming [B7,B8,B9], using discarded B8.\n- Also \"Peng J1\" \u2192 formed [J1,J1,J1].\n\nThus, your **three exposed melds** are:\n- [B2,B3,B4] (from Chi)\n- [J1,J1,J1] (from Peng)\n- [B7,B8,B9] (from Chi)\n\nYour current hand must consist of **only two tiles**, because a legal hand before draw is 13 tiles, and you form a winning hand when drawing the 14th.\n\nBut you list five tiles in hand. This suggests you haven't drawn yet \u2014 so current hand size should be 11 + 1 drawn = 12? No.\n\nStandard flow:\n- Start with 13 tiles.\n- Each time you Chi/Peng/Gang, you add a tile (from discard or wall), increasing hand by 1 temporarily, then discard one \u2014 net unchanged.\n\nYou\u2019ve made at least three such actions:\n- Chi B3 \u2192 added B3, discarded one\n- Peng J1 \u2192 added J1, discarded one\n- Chi B8 \u2192 added B8, discarded one\n\nEach time, you discard after melding, so hand size remains 13.\n\nAfter all actions, you should have:\n- 3 exposed melds \u00d7 3 tiles = 9 tiles exposed\n- 13 - 9 = 4 tiles in hand? But you show 5.\n\nInconsistency.\n\nWait \u2014 Kong would make it 4-tile meld. But none of your melds are Kongs.\n\nAll are Pungs/Chows of 3 tiles.\n\nTotal exposed: 3\u00d73=9 tiles.\n\nHand: 13 - 9 = 4 tiles.\n\nBut you list 5: T9, B2, B3, B4, B7 \u2014 impossible.\n\nConclusion: **There is data redundancy.** Likely, the system lists *all* tiles associated with you, including melded ones, but the \"hand\" refers only to concealed tiles.\n\nBut the prompt says: \"Your hand tiles are: [T9: 1; B2: 1; B3: 1; B4: 1; B7: 1]\" \u2014 this must mean **current concealed hand**.\n\nYet B2, B3, B4 were used in a meld \u2014 unless the meld was formed from different tiles?\n\nNo \u2014 you can't use same tile twice.\n\nUnless... you had duplicates.\n\nCheck counts:\n- B2: 4 total copies. Unshown: 1. Discarded: how many?\n  - You played B2 once (history: Play B2)\n  - Next player played B2\n  - Across player played B2\n  \u2192 At least 3 played. Only one left unshown \u2192 so yes, possible you had one in hand and others discarded.\n\nBut you also Chi\u2019d B3 to form [B2,B3,B4]. To do that, you must have held B2 and B4, and claimed B3.\n\nThen later, you played B2, B3, B4 individually \u2014 which makes sense: after melding, you still had extra singles.\n\nBut after forming [B2,B3,B4] via Chi, you expose those three tiles. Any additional B2/B3/B4 remain in hand.\n\nAh! So possibility: you had **extra copies**.\n\nFor example:\n- Held two B2s: used one in Chi, kept one.\nSame for B3, B4?\n\nBut each tile only has four copies.\n\nLet\u2019s verify feasibility.\n\nSuppose:\n- Total B2: 4 copies\n- You: used one in Chi, still hold one \u2192 two accounted\n- Discarded by others: next player and across player \u2192 two more \u2192 total 4 \u2192 okay.\n\nSimilarly B3:\n- You: used one in Chi, hold one\n- Discarded: you (Play B3), across (Play B3) \u2192 two more \u2192 total 4 \u2192 okay.\n\nB4:\n- You: used one in Chi, hold one\n- Discarded: you (Play B4), across (Play B4) \u2192 total 4 \u2192 okay.\n\nB7:\n- Used one in Chi [B7,B8,B9], so must have held B7 and B9, claimed B8\n- Still holding one B7? Possible only if had two initially.\n\nTotal B7: 4 copies\nUnshown: 3\nDiscarded: none visible in histories\nYou: used one in Chi, hold one \u2192 two\nSo two more unaccounted \u2192 possible.\n\nSo yes: you had duplicates.\n\nThus:\n- Your **exposed melds**:\n  - [B2,B3,B4] (via Chi)\n  - [J1,J1,J1] (via Peng)\n  - [B7,B8,B9] (via Chi)\n- Your **concealed hand**: [T9, B2, B3, B4, B7] \u2014 five tiles? Should be only four.\n\nWait \u2014 13-tile hand:\n- 3 exposed melds \u2192 9 tiles exposed\n- 13 - 9 = 4 concealed tiles\n\nBut you list 5 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nUnless you drew a tile already.\n\nCurrent turn: you are to discard.\n\nSo likely, you just drew a tile, making your hand 5 tiles (after drawing, before discarding).\n\nYes \u2014 correct sequence: draw \u2192 now 14 tiles \u2192 must discard one.\n\nSo before draw: 4 tiles in hand.\n\nAfter draw: 5 tiles \u2192 now choose one to discard.\n\nSo current hand: 5 tiles: [T9, B2, B3, B4, B7]\n\nExposed melds: [B2,B3,B4], [J1,J1,J1], [B7,B8,B9]\n\nNow, valid actions: [Play T9; Play B7; Play B3; Play B2; Play B4]\n\nSo you must discard one of these.\n\nWhich tile to discard?\n\nOptions:\n- **T9**: Terminal, only one in hand. T9 count: 2 unshown, some discarded (you played T9 earlier). Low risk.\n- **B7**: But you have [B7,B8,B9] meld \u2014 if you discard B7, you\u2019re giving away a tile from a completed meld. Opponents may suspect you don\u2019t need B7. But B7 unshown: 3, so not rare. Risk: could help someone with lower bamboo sequences or terminals.\n- **B2/B3/B4**: All are part of your exposed [B2,B3,B4] meld. Discarding extras is normal.\n\nBut consider safety:\n- **B2**: 3 copies already discarded (you x1, across x1, next x1), only 1 left unshown \u2192 extremely safe to discard.\n- **B3**: 2 discarded by others, you played one, and used one in meld \u2192 3 out, 1 in hand, 1 unshown \u2192 safe.\n- **B4**: similarly, high visibility \u2192 safe.\n- **T9**: two discarded (you x1, previous x1?), two unshown \u2192 somewhat safer than nothing.\n- **B7**: only one discarded (by you: Play B7), others not seen \u2192 higher danger.\n\nAlso, pattern considerations:\n- You have two Chows in Bamboos: [B2,B3,B4] and [B7,B8,B9]\n- Plus [J1,J1,J1]\n- And a T9 singleton\n- Concealed hand: T9, B2, B3, B4, B7\n\nWait \u2014 no: after drawing, you have 5 tiles. Which one did you draw?\n\nPossibilities: you drew T9, or one of the B-tiles.\n\nBut regardless, you must discard.\n\nNow, can you win?\n\nNot currently \u2014 no pair, no complete hand.\n\nAre you close to any high-scoring hand?\n\n- **Full Flush**: all Bamboos? No \u2014 you have T9 (Dots) and J1s (Honors) \u2192 not possible.\n- **Half Flush**: possible if you go for Bamboo + Honors \u2014 you have two Bamboo melds, one Honor meld, and mixed tiles.\n- But T9 is Dots \u2014 foreign suit.\n\nT9 is problematic \u2014 it's the only non-Bamboo/non-Honor tile.\n\nIf you keep T9, you can never achieve flush.\n\nBut can you form a pair with T9? Only if you draw another T9.\n\nT9 unshown: 2, so possible.\n\nBut better to **discard T9** to go for **Half Flush** with Bamboos and Honors.\n\nCurrently:\n- Melds: [B2,B3,B4], [B7,B8,B9], [J1,J1,J1]\n- Concealed: suppose you discard T9 \u2192 keep B2,B3,B4,B7\n\nBut you can only keep 4 tiles after discard.\n\nWait \u2014 after discard, back to 4 concealed.\n\nBest strategy: aim for **Half Flush** (8 points) or better.\n\nBut you already have three melds \u2014 need one more meld and a pair.\n\nYour concealed tiles are all singles \u2014 no pair.\n\nSo you need to draw a pair.\n\nAlternatively, wait for a tile to complete a fourth meld.\n\nBut your best chance is to **keep flexible tiles**.\n\nHowever, holding onto **T9** (Dots) limits you to mixed hands.\n\nWhereas **B2, B3, B4, B7** are all Bamboos \u2014 useful for potential pungs or chows.\n\nMoreover, **B2** appears frequently discarded \u2014 safest to play.\n\nBut **B4** and **B3** also widely seen.\n\nStill, **T9** is the only off-suit tile.\n\nTherefore, **discard T9** to commit to Half Flush.\n\nEven better: if you draw a Bamboo or Honor later, you can form another meld.\n\nAlso, **Seat Wind is North (F4)**, Prevalent Wind is North.\n\nBut you don\u2019t have F4.\n\nF4: all discarded (unshown: 0), so not available.\n\nJ1 you already have a pung.\n\nNo chance for Big/Little Winds.\n\nBut **Half Flush** is achievable: 6 points.\n\nOther patterns:\n- **All Pungs**: no, you have two Chows.\n- **Outside Hand**: all your melds involve terminals or honors?\n  - [B2,B3,B4]: internal \u2014 not outside\n  - So no.\n\nSo **Half Flush** is your main scoring hope.\n\nTo maximize chances, eliminate foreign suit.\n\nHence, **discard T9**.\n\nIt's also relatively safe \u2014 T9 not heavily sought.\n\nReason: Eliminates the only tile from a foreign suit (Dots), allowing pursuit of Half Flush (8 points), which requires only one more meld in Bamboos or Honors. T9 is the weakest link in hand coherence.\n\nAnswer: Play T9\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: Among the options, T9 is the only tile from a different suit (Dots), while all other tiles are Bamboos or part of existing Bamboo/Honor melds. Discarding T9 improves hand direction toward Half Flush. Other tiles like B2, B3, B4, B7 are all Bamboos and potentially useful for future pungs or chows. Although B2 is safest due to high exposure, removing the off-suit tile is strategically superior.\nReason: Playing T9 eliminates the foreign suit, aligning the hand toward Half Flush (8 points), a viable scoring pattern, and increases consistency in tile composition.\nAnswer: Play T9"}
