\n- T2, F2, F3, F4, J1 are heavily discarded \u2192 suggests opponents may have used them.\n\nYou already have two exposed pungs/kongs: [J1,J1,J1] and [B7,B8,B9], plus a chow [B2,B3,B4]. Your hand is nearly complete, needing only one more meld and a pair. But currently, you don\u2019t have a pair.\n\nNone of your valid actions create a new meld \u2014 all are discards. So the goal is to discard safely while keeping potential patterns open.\n\nAmong the options:\n- **B7** is part of an exposed chow and relatively safer due to B8/B9 exposure.\n- **J3** is an honor tile; with J1 punged and J2 frequently discarded, J3 might be less desired, but still risky as it completes the dragon set.\n- The **B2/B3/B4** group are mid-suit tiles likely to be dangerous.\n\nBut note: **you must discard one**, and your hand lacks a pair. You need to draw a pair candidate soon.\n\nAlso consider scoring potential:\n- If you later get a pair, you can aim for **Half Flush** (8 points) since your hand is mostly Dots + Honors.\n- You already have **Dragon Pung** (J1), worth 2 points.\n- With prevailing wind North (F4) and seat wind North, any **Pung of F4** would give Prevalent Wind (2 pts), but F4 has been discarded three times \u2014 unlikely.\n- F2, F3, F4 all heavily discarded \u2014 no wind pungs possible.\n\nBest safety comes from discarding a tile that is least likely to complete an opponent's hand.\n\nFrom unshown data:\n- B7: 3 unshown \u2014 relatively available, so less likely to be a single wait.\n- B2: 1 unshown \u2014 very few left; possibly dangerous.\n- B3: 2 unshown \u2014 medium.\n- B4: 2 unshown \u2014 medium.\n- J3: 2 unshown \u2014 but honor tiles are high-value; even one can win.\n\nHowever, looking at other players' melds:\n- Next player has [B1,B1,B1] \u2014 they cleared B1s, possibly going for terminals or all types.\n- Across has [T5,T6,T7], Previous has [T8,T8,T8], so T7 is safe.\n- No one has shown interest in Dots beyond low/mid.\n\nBut importantly: **B7** appears in your own exposed chow [B7,B8,B9], and B8/B9 have both been discarded \u2014 meaning **no one can complete B7-B8-B9 anymore**. Also, B6 is still around (2 unshown), but B5 is plentiful (3 unshown). So B7 could still be part of B5-B6-B7 or B6-B7-B8 \u2014 but B8 has been played twice already (by you and previous player), so B6-B7-B8 is risky for others.\n\nGiven all this, **B7 is safer than B2/B3/B4**, because:\n- B2/B3/B4 are central and highly combinable.\n- Many players build around 2-3-4, 3-4-5, etc.\n- B2, B3, B4 each appear in multiple chow combinations.\n\nIn contrast, **B7** is higher end, and paired with already-discarded B8/B9, its usefulness drops.\n\nStill, **J3** might be safest? Let's check:\n- J1: you punged it \u2192 known.\n- J2: discarded multiple times (by previous and next).\n- J3: only one seen (you played one), two unshown.\n- No one has Peng\u2019d or Gang\u2019d J3 \u2192 suggests no one has a pair.\n- So J3 may be relatively safe.\n\nBut honor tiles are often kept longer \u2014 so **J3 could be dangerous** if someone is waiting for dragons.\n\nCompare:\n- Discard **J3**: Risk giving away a dragon tile when someone might be completing Three Dragons.\n- Discard **B7**: Mid-high dot, but B8/B9 gone \u2192 lowers value.\n\nBut wait \u2014 you already *used* B7 in a meld. It\u2019s not in your hand anymore. Wait!\n\nWait \u2014 critical check:\n\nYour hand tiles are: [B2: 1; B3: 1; B4: 1; B7: 1; J3: 1]\n\nBut you also have melds: [[B2, B3, B4], [J1, J1, J1], [B7, B8, B9]]\n\nThis implies:\n- The **meld [B2,B3,B4]** is exposed \u2014 so those three tiles should not be in your hand.\n- Similarly, [B7,B8,B9] is exposed \u2014 so B7 should not be in hand.\n- And [J1,J1,J1] is exposed \u2014 so J1 not in hand.\n\nYet your hand shows B2, B3, B4, B7, J3 \u2014 this is inconsistent unless these are duplicates.\n\nCheck counts:\n- Each tile has max 4 copies.\n\nB2: In meld [B2,B3,B4] \u2192 uses one B2. One B2 in hand \u2192 total 2 B2s used. Unshown: 1 \u2192 total 3 accounted for \u2192 okay.\nSame for B3, B4 \u2014 plausible if you had extra.\n\nBut **B7**: You have [B7,B8,B9] meld \u2192 one B7 used. One B7 in hand \u2192 total two B7s. Unshown: 3 \u2192 fine.\n\nSo yes, you have an extra B7 in hand \u2014 meaning you could have two separate B7s.\n\nTherefore, **you are holding five tiles: B2, B3, B4, B7, J3**, and three exposed melds \u2192 standard 13-tile hand (3\u00d73 = 9, plus 4 in hand = 13).\n\nNow, since you have **both [B2,B3,B4] and individual B2/B3/B4**, that means you have **two copies** of each \u2014 e.g., B2\u00d72, B3\u00d72, B4\u00d72, etc.\n\nAh! That changes everything.\n\nYou actually have:\n- B2\u00d72 (one in meld, one in hand)\n- B3\u00d72\n- B4\u00d72\n- B7\u00d72 (one in [B7,B8,B9], one in hand)\n- J3\u00d71\n\nSo your hand is:\n- Melds: [B2,B3,B4], [B7,B8,B9], [J1,J1,J1]\n- Hand: B2, B3, B4, B7, J3\n\nThus, you have **doubles of B2, B3, B4, B7** \u2014 any of these could become pairs.\n\nMoreover, having four of a kind (e.g., two sets of B2) counts as **Tile Hog \u00d72** (since two different tiles have four copies? No \u2014 Tile Hog is per tile type with four copies not declared as Kong.\n\nDo you have four of any?\n\nLet\u2019s count how many of each you possess:\n- B2: 2 in play (1 in meld, 1 in hand) \u2192 only 2 \u2192 not four.\nSame for B3, B4, B7 \u2192 only 2 each.\n\nSo no Tile Hog yet.\n\nBut you could potentially promote a pair into a pung/kong later.\n\nBack to evaluation.\n\nSince you have multiple dot tiles, and your hand is strong toward **Half Flush** (dots + honors), and possibly **All Chows** if you form another chow and pair up.\n\nBut you currently have only one chow and two pungs \u2192 not All Chows.\n\nTo reach 8 points:\n- Already have: **Dragon Pung (J1)** = 2 points\n- If you get a pair, and win, depending on how:\n  - **Half Flush** = 6 points \u2192 total 8 \u2192 qualifies.\n  - **All Pungs** = 6 points \u2192 but you have a chow \u2192 invalid.\n  - **Melded Hand** = 6 points \u2192 possible if all melds from others, but you drew some.\n  - **Self Drawn** = 1 point.\n\nSo best path: aim for **Half Flush (6 pts) + Dragon Pung (2 pts) = 8 pts minimum**.\n\nFor Half Flush, you just need a pair in Dots or Honor.\n\nYou are drawing toward a pair \u2014 so protect potential pairs.\n\nCurrently, discarding any of B2, B3, B4, B7 breaks a potential pair (since you have one of each in hand).\n\nBut you only have one copy of each in hand \u2014 so discarding one leaves only one left (not a pair).\n\nExample: discard B2 \u2192 you have only one B2 left (in meld) \u2192 cannot make pair.\n\nSame for B3, B4, B7.\n\nSo whichever you discard, you lose chance to pair that tile.\n\nBut you must discard one.\n\nSo choose the **least dangerous** one to discard, i.e., the one most likely to be safe.\n\nNow assess danger:\n- **B2**: Needed for chows like B1-B2-B3, B2-B3-B4.\n  - B1: all discarded \u2192 cannot form B1-B2-B3.\n  - B2-B3-B4: you have a full meld of it \u2014 so you have at least one set. Another exists? Possibly.\n  - But B2-B3-B4 requires B2, B3, B4 \u2014 all are partially used.\n  - B2 unshown: only 1 left \u2192 so only one B2 remains unseen \u2192 low probability someone is waiting on it.\n- **B3**: Needed for B1-B2-B3, B2-B3-B4, B3-B4-B5.\n  - B1 gone \u2192 eliminates first.\n  - B2-B3-B4: possible, but B2 scarce.\n  - B3-B4-B5: B5 has 3 unshown \u2192 possible.\n  - But B3 unshown: 2 left \u2014 moderate danger.\n- **B4**: For B2-B3-B4, B3-B4-B5, B4-B5-B6.\n  - All possible, B5/B6 available.\n  - B4 unshown: 2 left \u2192 moderate.\n- **B7**: For B5-B6-B7, B6-B7-B8, B7-B8-B9.\n  - B8: 2 unshown, but already discarded (you played one, previous played one) \u2192 at least two out.\n  - B9: all discarded \u2192 so B7-B8-B9 impossible.\n  - B6-B7-B8: B6 has 2 unshown \u2192 possible.\n  - B5-B6-B7: B5 has 3 unshown \u2192 possible.\n  - But B7 unshown: 3 \u2192 relatively common, so less likely to be a rare wait.\n- **J3**: Honor tile. Could be used in:\n  - Big/Little Three Dragons\n  - All Honors\n  - Mixed hands\n  - No one has exposed J3 \u2192 so possibly someone is saving it.\n  - J1 punged (by you), J2 discarded multiple times \u2014 so Three Dragons needs J3.\n  - Therefore, **J3 is dangerous** \u2014 could complete Little/Big Three Dragons.\n\nThus, **J3 is the most dangerous discard**.\n\nSimilarly, B2 is safer due to B1 being gone.\n\nB7 is safer due to B8/B9 being exposed.\n\nBut B7 is in your meld [B7,B8,B9], and you have another in hand \u2014 so discarding B7 breaks a potential pair, but so does any other.\n\nBetween them, **B2** seems safest because:\n- B1 is fully discarded \u2192 removes many chow possibilities.\n- B2-B3-B4 is already melded by you \u2014 unlikely another player is building the exact same chow unless they have duplicates.\n- Opponents seem focused on other suits.\n\nAlternatively, **B7** might be better to keep because B8 and B9 are disappearing \u2014 so fewer chances for others to use B7.\n\nBut B7 is higher number \u2014 more likely to be involved in upper range chows.\n\nAfter weighing:\n- **J3** is too risky \u2014 avoid discarding.\n- Among B2/B3/B4/B7, **B2** is safest due to B1 absence.\n- **B4** is riskier because B3-B4-B5 and B4-B5-B6 are common mid-game chows.\n- **B3** similar.\n\nSo **B2** is the safest discard.\n\nWait \u2014 but you have **B2 in hand**, and you already used one in the meld \u2014 so discarding B2 means you have only one left (in the meld), so no pair possible.\n\nBut you must discard one \u2014 so you lose pairing chance anyway.\n\nFinal decision: **discard B2** \u2014 it's the least dangerous.\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include \"Play B2\", yes.\n\nHowever, reconsider: what about **B7**?\n\nYou have [B7,B8,B9] meld \u2014 so B7 is already used. Another B7 in hand \u2014 if you discard it, you lose pair chance, but B7 is more likely to be needed for chows than B2, since B2 relies on B1.\n\nBut B1 is gone \u2014 so **B2 is useless for 1-2-3 chow**.\n\nWhereas B7 can still go into 5-6-7, 6-7-8.\n\nSo **B2 is safer than B7**.\n\nYes.\n\nSimilarly, B3 and B4 are more versatile.\n\nSo safest tile to discard is **B2**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **valid actions** list: [Play B7; Play J3; Play B3; Play B2; Play B4]\n\nSo B2 is an option.\n\nBut earlier I thought B2 was safer \u2014 yes.\n\nHowever, let's cross-check with actual plays.\n\nAnother idea: **are you close to a pattern?**\n\nYou have:\n- Three melds: one chow, two pungs\n- Need one more meld and a pair\n\nIf you draw a pair, then you win.\n\nCurrently, you have singles of B2, B3, B4, B7, J3 \u2014 any could become a pair if you draw another.\n\nBut you must discard one \u2014 so reduce one possibility.\n\nBest to keep the one most likely to pair \u2014 i.e., most abundant in unshown.\n\nUnshown:\n- B2: 1\n- B3: 2\n- B4: 2\n- B7: 3\n- J3: 2\n\nSo **B7 has the most unshown copies (3)** \u2014 highest chance to draw another.\n\nTherefore, **keep B7**, discard a rarer one.\n\nB2 has only 1 unshown \u2014 lowest chance to pair.\n\nSo best to **discard B2** \u2014 both safest and least likely to be drawn again.\n\nThus, optimal choice: **Play B2**\n\nReason: B2 is the safest discard due to B1 being fully discarded (removing key chow combinations), and B2 has only one unshown copy left, making it unlikely to complete a pair if kept. Discarding it minimizes risk to opponents while sacrificing the least valuable pairing opportunity.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
