7, J3]\n- Melds: [B2,B3,B4], [J1,J1,J1], [B7,B8,B9]\n- So our structure is nearly complete \u2014 likely aiming for a standard four-meld + pair hand.\n- Possible waits: Could be waiting on B6 for a chow with B4,B5? Or B5 to pair?\nBut we already have four melds (three exposed), so we must be waiting on a pair or completing a new meld.\n\nWait analysis:\nWe need one more meld or pair. With [B4, B5\u00d72, B7, J3], possible pairs: B5\u00d72 is a pair candidate. Others are singles.\n\nSo most likely, we're waiting with B5 as the pair, needing one more tile to win.\n\nNow check safety:\n- **B4**: Has been Chowed before (next player did Chi B4), meaning they likely have B5/B6. Playing B4 risks letting them complete [B4,B5,B6].\n- **B5**: Less likely to be safe \u2014 if someone is building [B4,B5,B6], they may need B5. But since we have two, others may avoid it too. Still, danger exists.\n- **B7**: Already in a meld. Opponents may not be collecting high dots. No Chi involving B7 seen. Safer than B4/B5.\n- **J3**: Honor tile. Only two unshown. One in hand, two unshown \u2192 three total unseen. Previous players have discarded J2 and J3 \u2014 suggests honors are being cleared. Possibly safer.\n\nAlso consider scoring:\n- We have a pung of J1 (Dragon), and possibly going for **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 points) \u2014 but no second dragon pung.\n- We have three different suits? Yes: Dots, Bamboos, Characters \u2192 All Types? Not yet \u2014 no W pung. But we do have W chows discarded \u2014 no full meld.\n- Our hand is mostly dots \u2014 could go for **Half Flush** (honor + one suit): we have J1 pung, J3, and all other tiles in B. That\u2019s strong possibility.\n- **Half Flush** = 6 points.\n- Add **All Pungs**? No \u2014 we have chows (B2-B4 is a chow).\nWait \u2014 [B2,B3,B4] is a Chow, [B7,B8,B9] is a Chow, [J1,J1,J1] is a Pung \u2192 mixed meld types.\nSo not All Pungs.\n\nBut we have two chows in B \u2014 maybe going for **Lower Four / Upper Four**? B2-B4 is lower-middle,